\description{
Check whether age group labels meet conditions.
The \code{diagnose} functions report on each condition, and the
\code{assert} functions throw an error if a condition is not met.
\itemize{
\item \code{age_diagnose_values()} and \code{age_assert_values()} check the
elements of \code{labels}.
\item \code{age_diagnose_levels()} and \code{age_assert_levels()} check
the levels of \code{labels}, including any unused levels.
Both functions require \code{labels} to be a factor.
}

Conditions include:
\itemize{
\item Not overlapping
\item Not having gaps
\item Not having totals or missing values
\item Having, or not having, open age groups
\item Having an age group that starts at zero
\item Only including age groups used by an abridged life table
}
}
\section{Abridged and complete life tables}{

\itemize{
\item An abridged life table uses age groups \code{"0"}
and \code{"1-4"}, followed by 5-year age groups such as \code{"5-9"} and \code{"10-14"}.
\item A complete life table uses single-year age groups such as \code{"0"}, \code{"1"}, and \code{"2"}.
\item Both types of life table usually have an open age group
such as \code{"85+"} or \code{"100+"}.
}
}

\section{Reporting trees}{


In a reporting tree, records are tabulated at different levels of
detail. A census table, for instance, might have detailed
age groups, some broader age groups for records with less detailed
information, and a category for records with no information.
The age groups nest: any two either do not overlap, or one
contains the other. The most detailed age groups (the leaves of
the tree) cover the full span, so that every broader age group is
divided exactly into narrower ones. Leaves do not need to be
the same distance from the root: some broader age groups can be
divided further than others.

Some tables use a label such as \code{"Total"} to mean that the
age is not stated. Recode such labels to \code{"Missing"},
for instance using \code{x[age_is_total(x)] <- "Missing"},
so that they are not treated as totals (for instance
by \code{no_total}).

To check a complete age reporting tree, use \code{valid_tree = TRUE},
\code{has_zero = TRUE}, \code{has_open_right = TRUE}, and \code{no_total = TRUE}. To
check trees separately for each sex or date, use \code{\link[dplyr:group_by]{dplyr::group_by()}}.
}

